    {
      "id": "al14-l3",
      "title": "A Routine That Cleans Up",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "A polite subroutine: PUSH every pair it uses at the start, POP them in reverse order before RET. The caller never notices that the registers were borrowed.",
        "But careful: every PUSH needs exactly one POP before RET. Otherwise RET pops the wrong bytes and jumps somewhere random. That crashed many Altair programs.",
        "Results can still come back in A, if A isn't pushed."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "006   ; 000: MVI B",
          "062   ;      50",
          "076   ; 002: MVI A",
          "004   ;      4",
          "315   ; 004: CALL times8",
          "015   ;      address 015 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "323   ; 007: OUT 377",
          "377   ;      port 377",
          "170   ; 011: MOV A,B",
          "323   ; 012: OUT 377",
          "377   ;      port 377",
          "166   ; 014: HLT",
          "305   ; 015: PUSH B   ← times8",
          "006   ; 016: MVI B",
          "003   ;      3",
          "207   ; 020: ADD A   ← loop",
          "005   ; 021: DCR B",
          "302   ; 022: JNZ loop",
          "020   ;      address 020 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "301   ; 025: POP B",
          "311   ; 026: RET"
        ],
        "output": [
          "LEDS ○○●○○○○○ = 32",
          "LEDS ○○●●○○●○ = 50"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
